(function (root) { (root.NB || (root.NB = {})).SANDBOX_WORLD = {"bible":"# Wrenfold: A School for Late Magic — the story\n\n*The whole plan in plain prose, spoilers throughout. Start here. The world's details are in `02-world.md`, the people in `03-cast.md`, the opening film in `04-intro-storyboard.md`, and what ChatGPT needs to draw in `05-art-handoff.md`.*\n\n---\n\n## The book in one breath\n\nYou're twenty-five. You work a job you don't love in a rainy railway town called Wrexley, you live alone in a narrow house under the viaduct, and for most of this year small wrong things have been happening around you: bulbs blowing when you're angry, the kettle boiling before you've switched it on, candles leaning towards you like flowers to a window. At midnight on the last night of summer, a letter slides under your door with nobody on the other side of it. It says that your magic has begun, late, the way it does for one witch or wizard in four, and that a place has been kept for you at **Wrenfold**, the only school in the world for the late-kindled. Then a doorway of light draws itself in your kitchen wall.\n\nFrom September to Midsummer you learn to hold a wand, brew, fly, ward, read the stars and keep your own fire from burning you up, in an old castle across a black lake where ten thousand paper lanterns drift under the roof of the Lantern Hall. You're sorted by a lantern into one of four houses, you choose a familiar, you make friends and one of them, maybe, becomes more. And through all of it runs the dark: the **Grey Choir**, hooded singers who can hum a person's magic out of them like a candle, and who hunt the late-kindled above everyone else, because a late flame in its first year can be taken whole.\n\nEven the Headmistress can't keep them out. Even the Order of the Lamp can't. By Midsummer, you are the reason they're coming.\n\n---\n\n## Why a school for grown-ups\n\nMost witches and wizards kindle as children, somewhere between six and twelve; their magic is small and steady and their families teach them at home. About one in four kindles late, between twenty-one and thirty-five, all at once, and nobody knows why.\n\nA late flame is *bright and unset*. For about a year after it catches (**the Burning Year**) it surges, flares, and leaks: it can set a room alight, freeze a lake, or simply burn its owner out from the inside, leaving them grey and empty. Taught, it settles into something stronger than most early magic ever becomes.\n\nSo four hundred years ago **Hester Wren**, who kindled at thirty-one in a fishing village and nearly drowned a harbour, founded Wrenfold: one year, for anyone whose fire comes late. Students arrive at every age from twenty-one to thirty-four, from every kind of life: nurses, bakers, a firefighter, a law student, a mechanic, a disgraced heir, you. Nobody there was a child at school with anybody else. Everyone left something behind to come.\n\nA second year exists for the few who want it (Idris is in his). Most leave in June with their flame settled, a wand, a licence to practise, and a life to decide.\n\n---\n\n## Your gift: the Kindler\n\nIn the Burning Year every student's flame shows off a little: some brew as if they'd always known how, some can't stop things floating. Yours does something nobody's has done in forty years.\n\n**You can see flames.** Other people's magic shows to you as a light in the chest: a candle, a torch, a hearth, a spark. You can feel when one gutters. By October you learn you can **touch** them: steady one that's flaring, and (the thing that frightens the staff) **relight** one that has gone out. The old word is *Kindler*. Hester Wren was one. The last before you was a student called **Aldric Morrow**, forty years ago.\n\n**Limits, so the gift never solves things by itself:**\n\n- Seeing a flame tells you how strong it is and whether it's frightened or steady. It never tells you what anyone's thinking, whether they're lying, or how they feel about you.\n- Relighting costs you. Every flame you relight takes a little of yours, and a whole relighting leaves you cold and shaking for days. You can't relight everyone.\n- Hooded Choir-singers show as nothing at all: holes in the dark, as if they've hummed themselves out too.\n\nWho you tell is yours to decide. The Headmistress asks you to tell no one.\n\n---\n\n## The dark: the Grey Choir\n\nForty years ago, **Aldric Morrow** was the brightest student Wrenfold had seen in a century and the first Kindler since its founder. During a Warding exercise, a classmate's panic spell went wrong and **snuffed him**: put out his flame entirely. The school hushed it up: the classmate was from an important family, the Deputy of the day wanted no scandal, and a hollowed Kindler was an embarrassment. They sent Morrow home grey and empty and told him he'd recover.\n\nHe didn't. But he was still a Kindler somewhere under the ash, and he found that while he couldn't light his own flame, he could *take* someone else's and burn it for a while. A stolen flame lasts months. Then he needs another.\n\nHe has spent forty years learning to take flames without touching anyone: with a **note**. A hummed, wordless chord that makes flames gutter and lean towards it. He taught it to the lost and the bitter and the magicless-born who wanted what others had, and they became **the Grey Choir**. Choir members wear grey, hum rather than speak, and leave the people they take **hollowed**: alive, walking, polite, grey-skinned and blank, remembering less every week. The hollowed are kept at **St Ide's**, a hospital in the city nobody visits.\n\nLate flames in their Burning Year can be taken *whole*, in one song. That's why the Choir circles Wrenfold every year, and why the school has wards, the Lanternwarden's lanterns, and a Headmistress who doesn't sleep much.\n\n**What Morrow wants this year:** the **Heartfire**, Hester Wren's own flame, which she left burning under the school when she died. It lights every lantern in the Lantern Hall, holds the wards, and has kept Wrenfold safe for four hundred years. A Kindler could take it out of the rock and put it in a person. Morrow can't reach it himself: he needs a living Kindler to carry it. **He needs you.**\n\n**The Order of the Lamp** (the Lamplighters) exists to fight the Choir. They're brave, stretched thin, and not above using students as bait.\n\n---\n\n## The insider\n\nSomeone inside Wrenfold is helping the Choir: opening the wards a crack on certain nights, letting the humming in. It's **Absalom Tully, the Lanternwarden**, the gentle old man who tends the ten thousand lanterns and knows every student by name. His daughter **Maisie** was hollowed six years ago, at twenty-four, in her Burning Year. Morrow has promised to relight her once he has the Heartfire and a Kindler to carry it. Tully believes him.\n\nThe mystery points elsewhere first: at **Professor Magnus Grey**, the scarred and silent Warding master who was once a Lamplighter spy inside the Choir, and who has every reason to look guilty. He is innocent. He dies in February protecting students from the Choir, in the chapter called *The Quiet*, whatever you thought of him.\n\n---\n\n## The timeline\n\nOne school year, September to June, in 24 chapters. Placeless but British: rain, trains, chip shops, the Lantern Hall.\n\n### Part One: Kindling (September)\n\n**1. The Letter.** *(Thursday 10 September, night.)* The film opens it (see the storyboard): night over Wrexley, the viaduct, your lit window, the letter, the invitation, the doorway of light. Then the prose: who you are (name, witch or wizard, the life you've been living, the job), Nana Pearl across town, your friend Dev from work. The doorway will hold until dawn. You can pack one thing that matters, ring Nana, leave a note, or step straight through. You step through into Lamplight Row.\n\n**2. Lamplight Row.** *(10–11 September.)* A hidden street of all-night shops in the city of Kingsmere. The Wren's Nest welcome office and its fussy clerk Mr Fitch. Other new students: **Toby Quill** (a baker's apprentice who's been setting croissants on fire), **Rowan Ashby** (a firefighter who walked out of a burning house unburned), **Imogen Sallow** (a law student who read the whole handbook on the train), **Saoirse Maddock** (a motorbike mechanic who kindled by fixing an engine without touching it). A wand is chosen at Pellow & Daughters, where the wand chooses the hand. Robes at Hask & Needle. A familiar at the Menagerie, or a familiar chooses you. **The first shadow:** in a side alley, three grey-hooded figures hum, and every lamp on the Row gutters. A young Lamplighter, **Jory Penrose**, moves you all along too quickly.\n\n**3. The Lantern Hall.** *(Friday 11 September.)* The Lantern Train at dusk from Platform Nought; the boats across Wrenfold Mere in the dark; the castle; and the Hall itself: ten thousand paper lanterns drifting under a roof you can't see, humming faintly, candle-warm. **The Nesting:** each new student lights a lantern and lets it go, and it flies to one of four roosts. You can lean it. **Casimir Drummond**, the late-kindled son of an old family, is nested with an audible sneer. **Headmistress Imelda Kestrel's** speech: welcome, wonder, and one warning: *keep your flame close*.\n\n**4. First Lessons.** *(Mid-September.)* Wordcraft with tiny, delighted Professor Moth. Brewing with Professor Kovač, who says nothing twice. Turning with Professor Bassani, who says everything twice, theatrically. Flight on old school brooms with Coach Okoro. Warding with Professor Grey, who says almost nothing and whose hands are scarred to the wrist. Your flame surges in front of everyone. **Noor Haddad**, a nurse, sits with you after. The common room; your roommate; whose side you take when Cas humiliates Toby.\n\n**5. The Burning Year.** *(Late September.)* Night in the corridors. You see flames for the first time: the whole castle full of lights, and one guttering. You relight a snuffed candle with your bare hand, and **Idris Penhallow**, a second-year Kindling scholar, sees you do it. The Headmistress's study, the Weathervane Room: *you're a Kindler; tell no one*. A humming, deep in the walls, that nobody else can hear.\n\n**6. The Hollowed Girl.** *(Early October.)* **Delphine Arceneaux**, a Heronmere second-year, is found in the Glasshouses at dawn, grey and polite and empty. The school shakes. Detention (for being out after curfew) with the Lanternwarden, **Absalom Tully**, who shows you how the lanterns are fed and tells you about his daughter, in St Ide's. Her lantern: snuffed, with a grey wick.\n\n**7. An Evening Already Promised.** *(Saturday 10 October.)* The first Glimmerball match of the year, Rowan's first game; or Saoirse's forbidden workshop party in the Rookhallow Undercroft; or Noor's night shift in the Infirmary, which she asked you to sit through with her. What you promised, and to whom, matters. Toby asks **Priya** to the Emberfall lanterns.\n\n### Part Two: Emberfall (October to December)\n\n**8. Emberfall.** *(Saturday 31 October.)* The night the veil thins. Masks, apple-fire, lanterns floated on the Mere to the dead. Ghosts walk the cloisters for one night; one of them, **Lettice Crane**, hollowed forty years ago, walks straight up to you: *he's coming back for what he lost; you have it*. Imogen's brother is at St Ide's; she tells you, or doesn't.\n\n**9. Under Wrenfold.** *(Early November.)* A complete adventure: the Old Cloisters beneath the school are sealed, and something in them is calling students by name; or a painted map in the Owlcombe Stacks is eating the ink out of the library. Each is its own mystery with its own monster, and neither has anything to do with the Choir, except that both were woken by something moving the old wards.\n\n**10. The Lamplighters.** *(Mid-November.)* A second hollowing, a Rookhallow first-year, **Bram Hollis**, Cas's old crony. The Order arrives: **Commander Sabine Arkwright** and her officers take over the Warding Hall and interview everyone. Suspicion falls on Professor Grey (he was Choir once, as a spy), and on Cas (Bram's last argument was with him). Arkwright learns what you are, or doesn't, depending on who you've told.\n\n**11. Thimble Cross.** *(Saturday 5 December.)* The first village weekend: snow, the sweetshop, the Crooked Lantern, a wand-polishing stall, a present to buy. The Choir comes into the high street in daylight. **Odile Pellow**, the wandwright, who's opened a winter stall, stands in front of the students and sings the old counter-song until they hum her out. She's hollowed, not dead. It's the first time you see what the Choir does with your own eyes, and the first time you understand the Order can't be everywhere.\n\n**12. Longnight.** *(Friday 18 – Monday 21 December.)* The solstice: the Frost Market on the Mere, the Longnight Dance in the Lantern Hall (who you ask, who asks you), the feast at which every lantern burns blue at midnight. In the library, in Hester Wren's hand, the word *Heartfire*. Term ends. Go home for the holidays, or stay.\n\n### Part Three: Deep Winter (January to March)\n\n**13. Home Between.** *(Late December – early January.)* If you go home: Wrexley, Nana Pearl, Dev, your old job, your own bed, and how small and dear and strange it all is now. The Choir finds your street. If you stay: Wrenfold at Christmas, half-empty, snowed in, with whoever else stayed, and a night walk where you find the Heartfire's door, and something humming behind it.\n\n**14. Candlewake.** *(Tuesday 2 February.)* The festival of first light: every student carries a candle across the frozen Mere at dawn and gives it to someone. It means what you want it to mean. Route peaks. Idris shows you what he's been studying: Morrow's old school file, which someone removed from the Stacks.\n\n**15. The Glimmer Cup.** *(February.)* The inter-house tournament, in the snow. Sabotage: a lantern-hoop that snuffs the players who fly through it. Rowan falls. The saboteur used oil from the Lanternwarden's store. You notice, or you don't.\n\n**16. What the Founders Hid.** *(Late February.)* Hester Wren's journal, Morrow's file, the Warding exercise forty years ago, the classmate who snuffed him (the old Deputy's son: Casimir's grandfather), and the Heartfire under the school. What Morrow wants and why he needs a Kindler to carry it.\n\n**17. The Quiet.** *(Early March.)* The Choir comes inside Wrenfold at night through a ward someone opened. **Toby is hollowed.** **Professor Grey dies** holding a stair against six singers so the first-years can get out. The Headmistress is hurt. Nothing is safe, and the Order can't make it so.\n\n**18. The Lanternwarden.** *(Mid-March.)* The clues come together: the oil, the humming in the lanterns, the grey wicks, the ledger, St Ide's. It's Tully. What you do with him: expose him to Arkwright, go to the Headmistress, or go to St Ide's yourself and look at Maisie's flame.\n\n### Part Four: The Greening (March to June)\n\n**19. The Greening.** *(Saturday 20 March.)* Spring, and a choice. The Order offers to take you out of school and keep you safe, as bait, somewhere the Choir will follow. Or you stay. You try to relight Toby. It's the hardest thing you've done, and it costs you.\n\n**20. The Wren's Song.** *(April.)* The counter-song Odile Pellow sang, found in Hester Wren's journal, can't be sung by one person alone: it takes a choir of your own. You gather one, from all four houses, the people who'll stand with you because of how you've treated them all year.\n\n**21. Brightfire.** *(Saturday 1 May.)* The spring fire festival on the hill. The last good night: the bonfire, dancing, whoever you love. Then Tully's choice, or yours: the plan to use him to draw Morrow in.\n\n**22. Midsummer Eve.** *(Sunday 20 June.)* The Choir's assault during the Midsummer Proving. The lanterns gutter all at once. Battle in the Lantern Hall, the cloisters, the boathouse. Who stands where depends on everything you've done.\n\n**23. The Heartfire.** *(Night of 20–21 June.)* Under the school, in the root of the rock, the founding flame, and Aldric Morrow, grey as ash, asking you to carry it for him. What you do. The endings divide here.\n\n**24. The Last Lantern.** *(Saturday 26 June, and a year later.)* The Leaving Feast. What's lost and who's left. Where you go. An epilogue a year on, assembled from what you did.\n\n---\n\n## The endings\n\n| | Ending | How it comes |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | **The Heartfire Holds** | The Wren's Song holds the Hall; you keep the Heartfire from Morrow; the Order takes him. The school stands, and you keep your flame. |\n| B | **Every Lantern Lit** | You relight Morrow himself, his own flame, gone forty years. He becomes a tired old man who can do nothing but light a candle; the hollowed he took can be relit, slowly, by you and Idris. It costs you most of your fire. |\n| C | **The Cold Hall** | You keep the Heartfire from Morrow by letting it go out. The Hall goes dark, the wards fall, Wrenfold must move. Morrow dies of hunger in the root. Everyone lives. |\n| D | **The Last Lantern** | You give your own whole flame to relight the Heartfire after Morrow breaks it. Wrenfold stands. You go home to Wrexley without magic, remembering everything, and your friends come to visit. |\n| E | **The Grey Road** | You take the Heartfire yourself. Morrow was right about how it feels. |\n| F | **The Order's Oath** | Morrow escapes into the Fen with a stolen flame. You leave with the Lamplighters to hunt him. |\n| G | **What We Could Save** | You couldn't stop him, only choose who comes out of the root alive: Toby, Maisie Tully, or the Headmistress. Three variants, like Calder's. |\n| H | **The Road Not Taken** | You left in March with the Order, or went home in January. The last chapter is Midsummer read about in the paper, and a letter from whoever stayed. |\n\n---\n\n## The six (and Toby)\n\nRomance is open with any of the six leads, whatever you are, and entirely optional. Nobody has a sweetheart back home. Each route has five beats and a Midsummer promise, and a *together* or *parting* epilogue.\n\n- **Rowan Ashby** (26, Larkspire). Ex-firefighter from a big loud family. Walked out of a house fire unburned and hasn't been warm since. Brave to the point of stupid; terrified of his own flame, which comes out as heat. Route: learning to be afraid in front of someone.\n- **Imogen Sallow** (24, Owlcombe). Law student who read the handbook on the train. Sharp, ambitious, wants to join the Order the day she leaves. Her brother **Kit** was hollowed three years ago. Route: from using you to needing you.\n- **Saoirse Maddock** (28, Rookhallow). Motorbike mechanic, builds illegal contraptions, laughs at the wrong moments, broke every rule in her first week. Route: what she's avoiding by never sitting still.\n- **Casimir Drummond** (27, Rookhallow or Owlcombe, whichever you aren't). Heir to an old magical family, disgraced by kindling late, sneering, lonely, secretly kind. His grandfather is the man who snuffed Morrow. Route: rival to friend to more, and a family debt.\n- **Noor Haddad** (25, Heronmere). A&E nurse; calm, dry, good in a crisis, bad at being looked after. Kindled mid-shift by stopping a patient's bleeding with her hands. Route: letting someone carry something for once.\n- **Idris Penhallow** (29, Owlcombe, second year). The Kindling scholar studying the old Kindlers; the only person who sees what you are. Quiet, exact, secretive, suspected by half the school. Route: the one who knows your secret.\n- **Toby Quill** (23, Heronmere). Your best friend. Not a romance. Baker's apprentice, hapless, brave when it counts, in love with Priya. Hollowed in March. Whether and how he comes back is the heart of the last act.\n\n---\n\n## What carries across\n\n- Four stats: **Nerve**, **Wit**, **Heart**, **Flame** (raw power), 0–100, plus **Kindling** (how far your gift is trained) and **Chill** (what relighting has cost you, 0–3).\n- **House points** for all four houses, and the House Lantern at the Leaving Feast.\n- Hearts with the six leads (0–6) and friendship with twenty others (0–3).\n- Your **familiar**, chosen in Chapter 2, who is in every chapter and saves your life once.\n- Your **wand** (wood and heartspark), which says something about you.\n- **Evidence** towards the insider and Morrow's plan: 16 clues, each with two routes.\n- Promises: who you promised and whether you kept it.\n\n---\n\n## How it's kept consistent\n\nSame as Calder: `plan/` is the storyboard as checkable data (people, places, calendar, every scene with its date, place, cast and purpose), and the tools check the script against it: dates run forwards, nobody speaks before you meet them, the hollowed and the dead don't chat, everyone's where they could be. Bots play the game thousands of times to prove every option and ending is reachable.\n","people":[{"id":"rowan","name":"Rowan Ashby","age":26,"kind":"student","tier":"lead","knows":"","look":"Tall, broad-shouldered, open square face with a strong jaw and a slightly crooked nose (broken twice). Short copper-red hair, freckles everywhere, light skin that flushes easily. Warm brown eyes. Rolled-up sleeves, forea","limits":""},{"id":"imogen","name":"Imogen Sallow","age":24,"kind":"student","tier":"lead","knows":"","look":"Slight, very upright, heart-shaped face with a sharp chin and quick dark eyes behind thin gold glasses. Black hair in a severe bob with a blunt fringe; light olive skin. A tailored plum waistcoat under her robes; ink on ","limits":""},{"id":"saoirse","name":"Saoirse Maddock","age":28,"kind":"student","tier":"lead","knows":"","look":"Wiry, angular, freckled face with a wide grin that shows a chipped front tooth. Wild dark-auburn curls tied up with a bootlace; pale skin with oil smudges. Grey-green eyes. A leather jacket over her robes, goggles pushed","limits":""},{"id":"casimir","name":"Casimir Drummond","age":27,"kind":"student","tier":"lead","knows":"","look":"Tall and narrow, long elegant face with high cheekbones, a straight nose and a mouth set in a sneer by default. Pale blond hair swept back; very fair skin; cold grey eyes. Immaculate black robes with a copper-thread coll","limits":""},{"id":"noor","name":"Noor Haddad","age":25,"kind":"student","tier":"lead","knows":"","look":"Medium height, soft oval face with strong dark brows and calm, heavy-lidded brown eyes. Long dark hair in a thick practical plait; warm light-brown skin. A navy nurse's watch pinned to her sea-green-and-pearl robes; sens","limits":""},{"id":"idris","name":"Idris Penhallow","age":29,"kind":"student","tier":"lead","knows":"","look":"Lean, long face with deep-set dark eyes, a strong nose and a neat short black beard. Close-cropped black hair; deep brown skin. Round wire glasses. Plum-and-silver second-year robes worn soft, a satchel stuffed with note","limits":""},{"id":"toby","name":"Toby Quill","age":23,"kind":"student","tier":"frequent","knows":"","look":"Short and round-faced with pink cheeks, a snub nose and big anxious blue eyes. Floury-blond hair that sticks up at the crown; fair skin. A too-big knitted cardigan under his robes; flour on his sleeve.","limits":""},{"id":"imelda","name":"Imelda Kestrel","age":71,"kind":"staff","tier":"frequent","knows":"","look":"Tall, straight-backed old woman with a long lined face, bright hazel eyes and laughter lines. A silver braid to her waist, wound with a gold thread; brown freckled skin, star-freckles across the nose. Midnight-blue robes","limits":""},{"id":"aurelio","name":"Aurelio Bassani","age":58,"kind":"staff","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Round, theatrical, olive-skinned man with a magnificent curled grey moustache, bushy eyebrows and twinkling dark eyes. Bald on top with a grey fringe. Gold-and-rose waistcoat, a flower in his buttonhole that changes dail","limits":""},{"id":"vesna","name":"Vesna Kovač","age":49,"kind":"staff","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Thin, pale, angular face with sharp cheekbones and pale grey eyes that don't blink enough. Black hair with one white streak, pinned in a tight knot. High-necked plum robes, leather cuffs stained with potion.","limits":""},{"id":"mabli","name":"Mabli Rhys","age":52,"kind":"staff","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Sturdy, weathered, rosy-cheeked woman with a broad smile and crinkled green eyes. Grey-streaked brown hair under a battered wide-brimmed hat with a live snail on it; ruddy white skin. Earth under her nails, sea-green apr","limits":""},{"id":"tamsin","name":"Tamsin Crook","age":44,"kind":"staff","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Wiry woman with a wry, lopsided face, a pipe in the corner of her mouth and one eyebrow permanently raised. Short cropped silver hair; dark brown skin. Copper earrings, black leather gauntlet on one arm for birds.","limits":""},{"id":"magnus","name":"Magnus Grey","age":55,"kind":"staff","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Big, heavy, silent man with a craggy grey-stubbled face, a broken nose and deep-set tired blue eyes. Iron-grey hair cut short; pale weathered skin. Burn scars on both hands to the wrist; plain charcoal robes.","limits":""},{"id":"dunstan","name":"Dunstan Moth","age":67,"kind":"staff","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Tiny, delighted old man with a round pink face, enormous white eyebrows, huge round spectacles and a fluffy white beard. Stands on a stack of books to teach. Dusty lavender robes covered in chalk.","limits":""},{"id":"ines","name":"Ines Solano","age":38,"kind":"staff","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Dreamy woman with a long face, heavy-lidded dark eyes and a faint smile. Long black hair loose with silver stars tangled in it; light brown skin. Night-blue shawl sewn with constellations.","limits":""},{"id":"ferdie","name":"Ferdie Okoro","age":34,"kind":"staff","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Athletic, cheerful man with a wide grin, a whistle in his teeth and a shaved head. Deep brown skin. Flight jacket with house-coloured patches, goggles around his neck.","limits":""},{"id":"perpetua","name":"Perpetua Dunne","age":80,"kind":"staff","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Ancient, birdlike librarian with a pinched face, sharp black eyes and a lace collar. White hair in a bun stuck with pencils; papery white skin. A chain of reading glasses round her neck.","limits":""},{"id":"orrell","name":"Agnes Holloway","age":60,"kind":"staff","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Broad, brisk matron with a no-nonsense square face, apple cheeks and small shrewd eyes. Grey curls under a starched white cap; pink skin. Crisp white apron over pearl-grey robes.","limits":""},{"id":"absalom","name":"Absalom Tully","age":68,"kind":"staff","tier":"frequent","knows":"","look":"Stooped, gentle old man with a long kind face, a white walrus moustache and sad pale-blue eyes. Wisps of white hair under a flat cap; pink weathered skin. A patched brown coat, a long brass taper in his hand, oil on his ","limits":""},{"id":"priya","name":"Priya Menon","age":26,"kind":"student","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Tall, graceful, long face with a wide smile and bright dark eyes. Glossy black hair in a high ponytail; medium brown skin. Sea-green robes with the sleeves pushed up, a violin case on her back.","limits":""},{"id":"felicity","name":"Felicity 'Flick' Barrow","age":30,"kind":"student","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Brisk, sunny, round-faced woman with a turned-up nose and a clipboard. Blonde hair in two neat plaits; rosy white skin. Prefect's badge polished to a mirror.","limits":""},{"id":"marcus","name":"Marcus Oduya","age":27,"kind":"student","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Big, handsome, easy-grinning man with a strong jaw and a shaved fade. Dark brown skin. Glimmerball captain's jersey, broom over his shoulder.","limits":""},{"id":"petra","name":"Petra Lindqvist","age":25,"kind":"student","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Pale, cool, perfectly composed Scandinavian face with ice-blue eyes and white-blonde hair in a long braid. Silver cuff earrings.","limits":""},{"id":"hamish","name":"Hamish Galbraith","age":29,"kind":"student","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Gangly, grinning second-year with a long freckled face, a ginger beard and wild ginger hair. Pale skin. Kilt under his robes, a ferret on his shoulder.","limits":""},{"id":"delphine","name":"Delphine Arceneaux","age":31,"kind":"student","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Elegant woman with a long neck, fine features and dark amused eyes. Short natural hair dyed violet; deep brown skin. Sea-green second-year robes.","limits":""},{"id":"bram","name":"Bram Hollis","age":28,"kind":"student","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Thickset, sullen young man with a heavy brow, small eyes and a buzz cut. Ruddy white skin. Rookhallow black robes worn untidily.","limits":""},{"id":"mina","name":"Mina Achebe","age":24,"kind":"student","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Bright, fast-talking woman with a round face, huge headphones round her neck and gold hoop earrings. Braids piled high; dark brown skin. Plum robes covered in badges.","limits":""},{"id":"jonty","name":"Jonty Farthing","age":30,"kind":"student","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Huge, gentle ex-rugby player with a bashed-in nose, cauliflower ear and a shy smile. Sandy hair; pink white skin. Knits during lessons.","limits":""},{"id":"ptolemy","name":"Ptolemy Fitch","age":63,"kind":"row","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Thin, fussy clerk with a long nose, pince-nez, a quill behind each ear and a waistcoat covered in ink blots. Sparse grey hair combed over; sallow skin.","limits":""},{"id":"odile","name":"Odile Pellow","age":46,"kind":"row","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Striking woman with a strong face, dark knowing eyes and a wide mouth. Black curly hair shot with grey, tied in a scarf; olive skin. Leather apron with wand-wood shavings, a pencil through her bun.","limits":""},{"id":"madame","name":"Madame Hask","age":70,"kind":"row","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Small, sharp, elegant old woman with a pointed face, a mouthful of pins and a measuring tape round her neck that moves by itself. Silver bob; pale skin.","limits":""},{"id":"ambrose","name":"Ambrose Tick","age":55,"kind":"row","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Round, bearded, twinkly man with bushy eyebrows and straw in his hair. Ruddy brown skin. A waistcoat with pockets full of something wriggling.","limits":""},{"id":"sabine","name":"Sabine Arkwright","age":47,"kind":"order","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Hard, handsome woman with a scar through one eyebrow, a strong jaw and steady dark eyes. Close-cropped grey hair; brown skin. Long lamp-black greatcoat with a brass lamp badge.","limits":""},{"id":"jory","name":"Jory Penrose","age":24,"kind":"order","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Earnest young man with a boyish open face, big brown eyes and a nervous smile. Curly brown hair; light tan skin. A Lamplighter's greatcoat slightly too big for him.","limits":""},{"id":"aldric","name":"Aldric Morrow","age":66,"kind":"choir","tier":"frequent","knows":"","look":"Tall, gaunt old man with a long, once-beautiful face gone grey as ash, sunken cheeks and pale colourless eyes. Long white hair; skin with a grey tint. Grey hooded robes, bare grey hands, a wick-shaped iron pendant.","limits":""},{"id":"corliss","name":"Corliss Fane","age":40,"kind":"choir","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Tall woman with a hood, a pale severe face, closed lips and eyes rimmed grey. Shaved head; pale skin. Grey robes. She never speaks; she hums.","limits":""},{"id":"nana","name":"Nana Pearl","age":82,"kind":"ordinary","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Tiny, sharp-eyed grandmother with a round face, a pouf of white hair, big red-framed glasses and pink lipstick. Warm brown skin (or matching the player: drawn to suit the six looks). Cardigan, pearls, slippers.","limits":""},{"id":"dev","name":"Dev Mistry","age":26,"kind":"ordinary","tier":"supporting","knows":"","look":"Lanky, cheerful young man with a long face, big grin and messy black hair. Brown skin. A lanyard and a hoodie.","limits":""},{"id":"moll","name":"Moll Dunmore","age":57,"kind":"village","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Huge, cheerful landlady with a red face, a booming laugh and brawny arms. Grey hair piled up; rosy white skin. Apron with the Crooked Lantern stitched on it.","limits":""},{"id":"horace","name":"Horace Ombree","age":74,"kind":"village","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Plump, dapper old sweetshop owner with a pink bald head, a waxed white moustache and a striped bow tie. Light brown skin.","limits":""},{"id":"lettice","name":"Lettice Crane","age":25,"kind":"ghost","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"A translucent young woman in 1980s school robes, with a round sweet face, big grey eyes and a frizzy perm. Silvery-blue and see-through.","limits":""},{"id":"maisie","name":"Maisie Tully","age":30,"kind":"student","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Thin young woman with her father's long face and pale blue eyes gone foggy, fair hair in a hospital plait. Grey-tinged skin. A hospital cardigan.","limits":""},{"id":"kit","name":"Kit Sallow","age":27,"kind":"student","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Imogen's older brother: the same sharp chin and dark eyes, gone blank. Black hair grown out; light olive skin with a grey cast.","limits":""},{"id":"hester","name":"Hester Wren","age":60,"kind":"founder","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"A painted portrait: a weather-beaten fisherwoman with a broad face, a crooked smile and bright wren-brown eyes. Grey hair in a knot; sunburnt white skin. Oilskin coat. Painted in oils, cracked varnish, gilt frame.","limits":""},{"id":"honoria","name":"Honoria Drummond","age":88,"kind":"ordinary","tier":"minor","knows":"","look":"Formidable old woman with Cas's cheekbones, a hawk nose and cold pale eyes. White hair in an immaculate chignon; very fair skin. Black lace, jet beads.","limits":""}],"places":[{"id":"P01","name":"7 Viaduct Street","district":"Wrexley","notes":"Your narrow rented house under the railway viaduct: a kitchen with a yellow light, steep stairs, a front door with a draught under it, a back yard with one tomato plant. Trains shake the cups.","view":null},{"id":"P02","name":"Nana Pearl's bungalow","district":"Wrexley","notes":"Across town: lavender and toast, a budgie called Admiral, net curtains, a telly always on quietly, photographs of you at every age.","view":null},{"id":"P03","name":"Your work","district":"Wrexley","notes":"Wherever you worked (chosen in CH01): a call-centre floor, A&E reception, a pub kitchen, a department-store returns desk, the town library, a courier depot. Strip lights, a clock that's always slow.","view":null},{"id":"P05","name":"Lamplight Row","district":"Lamplight Row","notes":"A hidden street of all-night shops, reached through a door in a Kingsmere bus shelter: crooked gables, hanging lamps in every colour, cobbles, a smell of toffee and ozone, owls on the gutters.","view":null},{"id":"P06","name":"The Wren's Nest","district":"Lamplight Row","notes":"Wrenfold's welcome office: a tall narrow shopfront full of pigeonholes, forms that fold themselves, a kettle, a sleepy wren on the counter, and Mr Fitch.","view":null},{"id":"P07","name":"Pellow & Daughters, Wandwrights","district":"Lamplight Row","notes":"Drawers of wands to the ceiling, ladders, sawdust, a workbench with glowing shavings, a bell that rings before the door opens.","view":null},{"id":"P08","name":"Hask & Needle","district":"Lamplight Row","notes":"Bolts of cloth in house colours, mirrors that show you from behind, a gossiping tape measure.","view":null},{"id":"P09","name":"The Menagerie","district":"Lamplight Row","notes":"Familiars in baskets, cages with open doors, a sleepy fox, a hundred owls, hay, incense, a warm dark.","view":null},{"id":"P10","name":"The Lamp & Ladle","district":"Lamplight Row","notes":"All-night café-inn: soup in bowls the size of hubcaps, mismatched chairs, a fire, a cat on every table.","view":null},{"id":"P11","name":"Platform Nought","district":"Kingsmere","notes":"Under Kingsmere Central through a luggage office: a gaslit platform, the Lantern Train in green and gold, steam, trunks, familiars in carriers, goodbyes.","view":null},{"id":"P12","name":"Wrenfold Mere and the Boathouse","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"A long black lake in the hills; the castle on its wooded island; the old boathouse with lantern-boats that row themselves. Freezes at Longnight.","view":null},{"id":"P13","name":"The Lantern Hall","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Ten thousand paper lanterns drifting under a roof no one's seen; four long house tables; High Table under a great round window; the four roosts in the high corners. The heart of the school.","view":null},{"id":"P14","name":"Larkspire Tower","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Larkspire's round common room at the top of the east tower: cushions, gold-and-rose hangings, a balcony that catches the dawn, a piano nobody can play.","view":null},{"id":"P15","name":"The Owlcombe Stacks","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Owlcombe's common room under the Observatory: shelves and ladders, armchairs, a ceiling of real night sky, plum and silver.","view":null},{"id":"P16","name":"The Heronmere Cloister","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Heronmere's common room half under the Mere: green water-light through tall windows, fish going by, sea-green cushions, a still pool.","view":null},{"id":"P17","name":"The Rookhallow Undercroft","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Rookhallow's common room beneath the kitchens: warm stone, forges, workbenches, half-built contraptions, copper and black.","view":null},{"id":"P18","name":"The Wordcraft Gallery","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"A long classroom with tall windows, desks carved with four hundred years of initials, chalk spells on the board, floating feathers.","view":null},{"id":"P19","name":"The Brewing Cellars","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Vaulted cellars, cauldrons over blue fires, jars of things, steam, Professor Kovač's silence.","view":null},{"id":"P20","name":"The Glasshouses","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Victorian glasshouses on the south lawn: grumbles in pots, candle-moss, a vine that hums, fogged glass.","view":null},{"id":"P21","name":"The Observatory","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"A brass-domed tower with a great telescope, star charts, a slot of night sky.","view":null},{"id":"P22","name":"The Long Stacks","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"The library: a long galleried hall of books, rolling ladders, green lamps, a restricted cage, Miss Dunne.","view":null},{"id":"P23","name":"The Warding Hall","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"A stone hall scorched by four centuries of practice spells; training circles on the floor; shields on the walls. The Order's headquarters from November.","view":null},{"id":"P24","name":"The Infirmary","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Long white ward with iron beds, screens, a fire, Matron's desk, bottles that glow.","view":null},{"id":"P25","name":"The Weathervane Room","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"The Headmistress's round study at the top of the central tower: weather instruments, a hundred clocks, a sleeping kestrel, Hester Wren's portrait.","view":null},{"id":"P26","name":"The Rookery","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"The familiar tower: perches, baskets, straw, a hundred animals, windows open to the wind.","view":null},{"id":"P27","name":"The Glimmer Pitch","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Over the Mere's south shallows: floating lantern-hoops, stands of old timber, house banners.","view":null},{"id":"P28","name":"The Lanternwarden's Cottage","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"A crooked stone cottage by the boathouse: oil drums, a hundred tapers, spare lanterns drying on lines, a kettle, a photograph of a girl.","view":null},{"id":"P29","name":"The Old Cloisters","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Sealed cloisters under the school: dripping stone, old wards scratched on pillars, locked doors, something that calls names.","view":null},{"id":"P30","name":"The Heartfire Chamber","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"At the root of the rock beneath the school: a cavern where Hester Wren's flame burns in a crack in the stone, white-gold, and every lantern's thread runs up from it.","view":null},{"id":"P31","name":"The Whispering Wood","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Old forest on the island's north end: silver birches, whispering leaves, will-o'-lights, things that watch.","view":null},{"id":"P32","name":"Wrenfold's corridors","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Stone corridors and stairs that rearrange when bored, portraits that gossip, suits of armour with nesting birds, lanterns in brackets.","view":null},{"id":"P33","name":"The Kitchens","district":"Wrenfold","notes":"Huge warm kitchens: copper pans, bread ovens, a cook called Mrs Pettigrew and her goat.","view":null},{"id":"P34","name":"Thimble Cross","district":"Thimble Cross","notes":"The village at the south end of the Mere: a crooked high street, a green, a market cross, snow on everything in December.","view":null},{"id":"P35","name":"Sugar & Sorcery","district":"Thimble Cross","notes":"The sweetshop: jars to the ceiling, humming humbugs, fizzing moonstones, chocolate wrens that flap.","view":null},{"id":"P36","name":"The Crooked Lantern","district":"Thimble Cross","notes":"The pub: low beams, a roaring fire, butterbeer-cousin 'hearthale', Moll Dunmore.","view":null},{"id":"P37","name":"The Candlestones","district":"Thimble Cross","notes":"A ring of standing stones on the hill above the village, where the Brightfire burns in May.","view":null},{"id":"P38","name":"The Lamplighters' House","district":"Kingsmere","notes":"The Order's headquarters in Kingsmere: a tall black townhouse with a lamp over the door that never goes out; maps, briefings, tired people.","view":null},{"id":"P39","name":"St Ide's","district":"Kingsmere","notes":"The hospital for the hollowed: a quiet Victorian building, long pale wards, patients sitting very still by the windows, visitors' chairs nobody uses.","view":null},{"id":"P40","name":"The drowned chapel, Saltmarrow Fen","district":"The Fen","notes":"A half-sunk chapel in the marshes: grey reeds, black water, a roof open to the sky, candles that burn grey. The Choir's gathering place.","view":null}],"moods":["neutral","attentive","amused","laugh","warm","shy","tense","scared","angry","hurt","sad","guarded","surprised","tired","small","moon","hungry","returned"]}; })(typeof window !== "undefined" ? window : globalThis);
